joined now by the shadow of justice  secretary steve reed for some more on  this steve uh good morning to you first  of all  let's just pick up if we can on those  price rises i mean we've seen some ideas  coming in as how to deal with it do you  support this idea of just not bringing  that price right in when the new price  cap is made on the 26th of october  sorry labour's going to bring forward a  full package of proposals in the next  few days so we'll see entirely what our  package will be but the point is we've  got a zombie government that's gone slow  at every single stage of this growing  crisis over the last over the last year  labour proposed a windfall tax on the  surplus profits of the energy companies  nearly a year ago initially the  government said they would never do that  and then the u-turned but they haven't  done that properly because it turns out  there's a great big loophole in the  middle of it and for every pound that  the energy companies are investing they  get a 91p subsidy back from the taxpayer  now that is money and it will amount to  billions of pounds that is money that  could be being used to help families who  are right now facing seeing their energy  bills rocket  uh and to an eye-watering extent from  about a thousand pounds on average a  year ago we just saw the figures that  you put up on the screen there the  projection for next april is that they  will hit four and a half thousand pounds  on average now millions of families are  desperately worried that they will be  going into debt just to pay their energy  bills over this winter and instead of  doing anything to help this government  is uh they've got soon i can trust  fighting each other like rats in a sack  and the government doing absolutely  nothing about coming up with proposals  for how they can help people face this  crisis which is looming and terrifying  families families are already in debt  because of the bills that that they're  facing and you say that the labour party  is going to come forward with some  proposals in the next few days i've been  asking a week for you guys to come  forward with proposals but gordon brown  seems to i mean he's is he the leader  for the period while securities  it's always interesting to hear what  gordon brown has got to say he's a very  senior  figure from you know his former labour  former labour prime minister as i said  leading your policy right now this is  when we're going through this crisis  well we'll see our policy over the next  few days that'll be brought out in in  full the the key problem here is that  we've got a government that seems to be  like a zombie government it's time they  got off up this off the slab and started  focusing on what they can do to help the  british people right now but instead of  that labor will be coming up with our  proposals next week trust and sunnac are  saying they're going to do nothing not  even look at it until september what is  the point of having a government if  they're not going to help through a  crisis like this  well look the point being made by gordon  brown is that crisis don't take holidays  secure starman is on holiday and he's  the one who's leading the charge for the  labour party right now no labour's going  to come come up with a fully costed  package of proposals for how we will  help british people within the next few  days next week is when we'll bring that  forward the government the government  are meeting the you know the  chancellor's meeting the energy  companies today and bizarrely they've  been trailing that they're not going to  take any decisions from that meeting one  of the things they could do is to close  that tax loophole and instead of  subsidizing energy companies that are  making so much money they don't know  what to do with it use that money to  give help to the british people now  steve did you write you know that  loophole as you call it is they say it's  to make sure that these companies keep  investing and we'll we don't need to  it's nothing of the sort is it because  their investment decisions decisions are  taken years in advance so all they're  doing is subsidizing them for decisions  they took years ago that money should be  helping british people if your your  energy bill is going to go up from a  thousand pounds to four and a half  thousand pounds in six months how on  earth are people going to pay that  you're looking at nearly a third of the  british people going into debt because  of this we need the government to focus  on this problem not watch soon i can  trust fight each other likes rats in a  sack when we should be having a  government that's focused on the  problems of the british people so the  labor party is going to come with a  package of proposal would one of them be  to renationalize these companies if the  price keeps rising that is that i mean  that's something that um rachel reeves  and kierstarma have both said we're not  looking at at the moment i mean if  there's a if there is money available  then we need to be using it to help  british people right now nationalizing  companies costs an awful lot of money  i'd rather that we're looking at what we  can do to help british people and we  have to do that responsibly within the  funding that that is available there  will as i say be a full package brought  forward in the next few days you'll hear  more about it um over the weekend i'm  sure you'll have me or my colleagues on  the show i hope so talk about it but  what you won't have is anyone from the  government saying what they're going to  do because they intend to sit on their  hands for another month i've already  given the government just time about  this and i will continue to do that  throughout the day if i can let's move  on to strike action now the labour party  of course have in a difficult position  when it comes to strikes there's the  a strike from postal workers we've got a  barrister strike ongoing at the moment  where do you stand on the british  striker shadow justice secretary would  you join them on the pickup well i don't  i don't see why you say the labour party  is in a difficult position on the  strikes because because i think a lot of  unions who are backing the strikes who  have balloted their members would like  to see the labour party full-throatedly  backing the workers and fighting for  them to get i'm a former trade union  shop steward uh myself um the role of a  trade union is to get out there and to  fight for their workers including on the  picket line when their workers choose to  go on strike the labour party is now  preparing to enter government it is not  our job to stop the british people going  to work it is our job to demonstrate  that we fully understand the immense  pressures that this tor regenerated cost  of living crisis is putting on household  budgets and demonstrate what we can do  to help now in terms of the strikes  themselves i've been absolutely  astonished that the government has just  sat back and refused to get involved and  to try and help negotiate a solution to  the problems whether it's the trains  whether it's the barristers time and  time again they just step back nothing  to do with me gov you know what's the  point of having a government if they're  not going to help with the energy crisis  and they're not going to help with the  cost of living crisis if they're not  going to help resolve the strikes that  are happening frankly because the  government has allowed this um cost of  living crisis to spiral out of control  it's the government that needs to be  doing things uh right now labour will be  demonstrating next week that we have the  ideas and the plan to help the british  people while the government is doing  absolutely diddly squat right steve read  shadow justice actually look forward to  hearing those ideas next week you said  absolutely next few days next week  all right thank you very much

